# Question 1 — Object Reference vs Shallow Copy

### Code

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Rahul",
    address: {
        city: "Ahmedabad",
        pincode: 380001
    }
};

const admin = { ...user };

admin.name = "Amit";
admin.address.city = "Mumbai";

console.log(user);
console.log(admin);
console.log(user === admin);
console.log(user.address === admin.address);
```

### Your answer

> user will remain same but address will be changed, admin name and city will be changed
> false
> true
> object inside object will point to same reference

### Correct answer

```javascript
console.log(user);
// {
//   name: "Rahul",
//   address: {
//     city: "Mumbai",
//     pincode: 380001
//   }
// }

console.log(admin);
// {
//   name: "Amit",
//   address: {
//     city: "Mumbai",
//     pincode: 380001
//   }
// }

console.log(user === admin);
// false

console.log(user.address === admin.address);
// true
```

### Why?

The spread operator creates a **shallow copy**.

```javascript
const admin = { ...user };
```

The outer object is new:

```text
user  ───► Object A
admin ───► Object B
```

Therefore:

```javascript
user === admin
// false
```

But the nested `address` object is not copied.

Both point to the same nested object:

```text
user.address  ──┐
                ├──► Address Object
admin.address ──┘
```

Therefore:

```javascript
user.address === admin.address
// true
```

So:

```javascript
admin.address.city = "Mumbai";
```

also changes:

```javascript
user.address.city
```

because both references point to the same object.

### Production-level concept

> Spread syntax performs a shallow copy. Primitive properties are copied by value, while object/array properties retain their references.

### Deep copy

Modern JavaScript provides:

```javascript
const copy = structuredClone(user);
```

Now:

```javascript
copy.address.city = "Mumbai";

console.log(user.address.city);
// Ahmedabad
```

because the nested object has also been cloned.

