# Question 2 — `this` in Object Methods

### Code

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Bhavya",

    regularFunction: function () {
        console.log(this.name);
    },

    arrowFunction: () => {
        console.log(this.name);
    }
};

user.regularFunction();
user.arrowFunction();
```

### Your answer

> 1. bhavya
> 2. undefined
> 3. first one is normal function while other one is arrow function which doesn't have own this and uses global

### Correct answer

```text
Bhavya
undefined
```

### Regular function

```javascript
user.regularFunction();
```

For a normal function, `this` depends on the **call site**.

Because the function is called as:

```javascript
user.regularFunction();
```

the object before `.` becomes `this`.

Therefore:

```javascript
this === user
```

and:

```javascript
this.name
```

is:

```text
Bhavya
```

### Arrow function

Arrow functions do **not** have their own `this`.

```javascript
arrowFunction: () => {
    console.log(this.name);
}
```

It gets `this` lexically from its surrounding scope.

So don't say in an interview:

> "Arrow functions always use global this."

The better answer is:

> Arrow functions don't have their own `this`; they inherit `this` from their surrounding lexical environment.

The actual value depends on the surrounding context.

