# Question 3 — Losing `this`

### Code

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Bhavya",

    regularFunction: function () {
        console.log(this.name);
    }
};

user.regularFunction();

const fn = user.regularFunction;

fn();
```

### What happens?

First:

```javascript
user.regularFunction();
```

prints:

```text
Bhavya
```

But after:

```javascript
const fn = user.regularFunction;
fn();
```

the function is no longer called through `user`.

The call changed from:

```javascript
user.regularFunction()
```

to:

```javascript
fn()
```

Therefore the original object is no longer the receiver.

In strict mode:

```javascript
this === undefined
```

and attempting:

```javascript
this.name
```

can produce:

```text
TypeError
```

### Production importance

This is a common issue when passing methods as:

* callbacks
* event handlers
* timers
* framework callbacks

Possible solutions include:

```javascript
fn.bind(user);
```

or using an arrow wrapper when appropriate.

