# ⭐ Most Important Object Interview Rules

Remember these:

### 1. Assignment doesn't copy an object

```javascript
const a = obj;
```

Both point to the same object.

---

### 2. Spread creates a shallow copy

```javascript
const b = { ...obj };
```

New outer object, but nested references may remain shared.

---

### 3. `structuredClone()` can create deep copies

```javascript
const b = structuredClone(obj);
```

But it has limitations and isn't a universal clone for every JavaScript value.

---

### 4. `===` compares object references

```javascript
{} === {}
// false
```

because they are two different objects.

```javascript
const a = {};
const b = a;

a === b;
// true
```

---

### 5. Regular function `this`

Usually determined by **how the function is called**.

```javascript
obj.method();
```

→ `this` is `obj`.

---

### 6. Arrow function `this`

Arrow functions don't have their own `this`.

They use lexical `this`.

---

### 7. `hasOwn` vs `in`

```javascript
Object.hasOwn(obj, "x")
```

checks own properties.

```javascript
"x" in obj
```

checks own + inherited properties.

---

### 8. `Object.freeze()` is shallow

```javascript
Object.freeze(obj);
```

doesn't automatically freeze nested objects.

---

### 9. Prototype chain

If JavaScript can't find a property directly on an object, it searches its prototype chain.

```text
object
   ↓
prototype
   ↓
prototype's prototype
   ↓
...
```

---

### 10. Destructuring defaults

```javascript
const { x = 10 } = obj;
```

uses `10` when `x` is `undefined`, not when it is `null`.

