# 34. Your Object Production-Level Question From Today

The latest question I gave you was:

```js
const user = {
    name: "Bhavya",
    age: 21,
    address: {
        city: "Ahmedabad"
    }
};

const admin = { ...user };

admin.name = "Rahul";
admin.address.city = "Mumbai";

console.log(user);
console.log(admin);
console.log(user === admin);
console.log(user.address === admin.address);
```

### Correct answer

`user`:

```js
{
    name: "Bhavya",
    age: 21,
    address: {
        city: "Mumbai"
    }
}
```

`admin`:

```js
{
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 21,
    address: {
        city: "Mumbai"
    }
}
```

Then:

```js
user === admin
```

→

```text
false
```

But:

```js
user.address === admin.address
```

→

```text
true
```

### Why?

Spread:

```js
const admin = { ...user };
```

creates a **new outer object**, but the nested `address` object is still shared.

